    "display_name": "Jade's Boba Shop",
    "long_instructions": "If tomorrow afternoon hits 85 degrees, how many drinks should Jade prepare for?\r\nThe client’s records are in the program: afternoon temperature (℉), and drinks sold, for five days of records. Right now the program stops with an error.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Run this program and notice the error in the console. Consider why the error might be happening.\r\n- `scikit-learn` requires that each input example is wrapped in its own list, like `[[1], [2], [3]]`. Rewrite temperatures as a list of lists, then run the program again.\r\n- Make the prediction you made on paper: `value = model.predict([[85]])[0]`, then print it with `print(f\"Prediction for 85: {value}\")`.\r\n- If time allows, discuss with your partner: if the client asked you to predict for 104 degrees, could your paper plot have answered that?\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\n`model.fit(inputs, outputs)` trains the model, and scikit-learn expects every input wrapped in its own list. `model.predict([[85]])` returns a list holding one prediction, so `[0]` takes the number out of it.\r\n",
